|> Exercise
    !! work=ing
    handle=_relate_general_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_polar_conversion_to_cosx_plus_sinx_

    Relate Exercise >>_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_ to Exercise >>_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_.

    !! Relate the solution of Exercise >>_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_ to the result of Exercise >>_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_,
    !! which, to recall the latter, is the fact that
    !! $
    !! \cos x + \sin x = \sqrt{2}\cos(x - \eta/2)
    !! $
    !! for all $x \in \rr$.

    !! Does Exercise >>_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_ relate to Exercise >>_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_?

    !! Does Exercise >>_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_ relate to Exercise >>_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_?

    !! In Exercise >>_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_, which $W$ and $\psi$ are obtained
    !! if $A = B = 1$?

    |> Solution

        To recall, Exercise >>_cosx_plus_sinx_as_sinusoid_
        establishes that

        $$
        \cos x + \sin x = \sqrt{2}\cos(x - \eta/2)
        $$

        but this is the special case 
        $$
        A = B = 1
        $$
        of
        Exercise >>_Acosx_plus_Bsinx_as_sinusoid_ since

        $$
        \cos x + \sin x = 1\cdot \cos x + 1\cdot \sin x
        $$

        and since

        $$
        (\sqrt{2}, \eta/2)
        $$

        are the (“the”) polar coordinates of the point $(1, 1) \in \rr^2$!